@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Determinization — Perfect-Information Monte-Carlo over Sampled Worlds
' Paper: GIB: Imperfect Information in a Computationally Challenging Game
' URL:   https://doi.org/10.1613/jair.820
' Level: 1

database  "Belief\nposterior over hidden state" as BEL <<db>>
component "Sample K worlds\neach consistent with the belief" as SAMPLE <<async>>
component "Perfect-information solve\nexisting evaluator per world" as SOLVE <<app>>
component "Aggregate per candidate\naverage across worlds" as AGG <<host>>
component "Chosen action\nbest expected value" as ACT <<app>>

BEL ARROW_QUEUE SAMPLE : the sampler //is// the belief model
SAMPLE ARROW_MAIN SOLVE : world 1..K
SOLVE ARROW_MAIN AGG : score every legal candidate
AGG ARROW_MAIN ACT

note bottom of SAMPLE
  **Why it is the cheapest first experiment.** It
  reuses a perfect-information evaluator unchanged;
  the only new machinery is a sampler that draws
  worlds consistent with what this side can actually
  know. A system already tracking possible-piece
  sets and posteriors has that sampler.
  ----
  **Its known blind spots, by their literature names.**
  //Strategy fusion//: the search may pick a different
  action in each sampled world, implicitly assuming it
  can behave differently in states it cannot actually
  distinguish. //Non-locality//: values propagated from
  a fully-observable solve ignore that the opponent's
  play depends on information the agent does not have.
  (Both named by Frank & Basin.) Consequently the
  agent plans neither to //gather// information nor to
  //conceal// it — in every sampled world it already
  knows everything. Where that matters, the
  equilibrium methods in
  ""counterfactual-regret-minimization.puml"" are the
  honest answer.
end note
@enduml
